title: add() double-qualifies literal domain-qualified provider map keys
updated: 2026-08-31T19:04:06Z
---

IO::K8s::add() treats every provider resource_map key as a bare Kind and
runs it through _qualify_class_path(), so a key that is ALREADY
domain-qualified gets qualified a second time.

Repro (measured on master, pre-existing -- not introduced by karr #58):

  IO::K8s->new(with => ["IO::K8s::GatewayAPI"])->resource_map
  --> gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1/gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1/ReferenceGrant
      => GatewayAPI::V1::ReferenceGrant

The GatewayAPI ReferenceGrant entry established the literal-qualified-key
pattern; karr #58 added four more such keys to IO::K8s::AgentSandbox, so
the merged map now carries five junk entries of that shape.

Harmless at runtime: no lookup path can produce a name of that shape, so
nothing resolves through them. But they pollute resource_map for anyone
dumping or iterating it, and any count-based assertion over the merged map
is off by the number of qualified provider keys.

Fix sketch: in add()/_qualify_class_path (lib/IO/K8s.pm), skip qualification
for keys that already contain a "/" -- they are exact GVK requests and are
meant to be registered verbatim.

Found by the sandbox lane while implementing karr #58 (repro verified by
the orchestrating agent).
